.TH WB\-PROMPT 1 "" "watchbill"
.SH NAME
wb\-prompt \- print the prompt of a role
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwb\fR prompt <role> [<issue>] [\-\-issue <text>] [\-\-change <text>]
    [\-\-requirements <text>] [\-\-round <text>] [\-\-checkout <text>]
    [\-\-branch <text>] [\-\-issue_address <text>]
    [\-\-change_address <text>] [\-\-classed <text>]
    [\-\-classed_touched <text>] [\-\-description <text>]
    [\-\-brought <text>] [\-\-questions <text>]
    [\-\-change_comment <text>] [\-\-needs_person <text>]
    [\-\-runner <name>] [\-\-parts] [\-\-stamp] [\-\-show\-origin] [\-q | \-\-quiet]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Print the prompt of a role on standard output and nothing else: the role's text of the pinned revision with each placeholder filled from the option of its name, such as \-\-issue for {issue}, then the project's own text. The issue is the word after the role, or \-\-issue. What no option gives is taken from the repository and watchbill.yml: the checkout, the branch, the two addresses and the requirements; an option always wins.
.PP
It runs the check of wb check first and prints nothing when that fails. \-\-parts prints the path and the SHA\-256 of each part, in the form sha256sum prints, in place of the text. \-\-stamp prints, in place of the text, what the assignment is: the role, its model and effort, the runner, the version and revision, the issue, the change, the round, the branch, the checkout, the time, and each part as \-\-parts gives it.
.PP
Not with \-\-parts. \-\-show\-origin prints the whole prompt as it is, and before each part a line that says its kind (revision, issue, project), its path, where it was read from and its hash, the hash of its part line of the stamp. Below that line stands one line for each placeholder the part holds, and where its value came from. Not with \-\-parts or \-\-stamp.
.PP
\-\-runner names the runner the prompt is handed to. The planner needs it, and is handed out only to a runner that /etc/watchbill/organisation.yml clears for the planner up to the level of the repository, the line confidentiality: of watchbill.yml.
.PP
Its stamp then has the model and effort of that file, and \-\-parts adds the level and the runner. Ends with 0 when the prompt is printed, 1 when it is refused, 2 when it was used wrongly or the repository is not set up. Writes nothing.
.PP
\-q (\-\-quiet) is taken and changes nothing: what it prints is what a script reads.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-issue <text>
The issue, for {issue}. It is also the word after the role.
.TP
.B \-\-change <text>
The change, for {change}.
.TP
.B \-\-requirements <text>
The requirements, for {requirements}.
.TP
.B \-\-round <text>
The round, for {round}.
.TP
.B \-\-checkout <text>
The checkout, for {checkout}.
.TP
.B \-\-branch <text>
The branch, for {branch}.
.TP
.B \-\-issue_address <text>
The address of the issue, for {issue_address}.
.TP
.B \-\-change_address <text>
The address of the change, for {change_address}.
.TP
.B \-\-classed <text>
The classed paths, for {classed}.
.TP
.B \-\-classed_touched <text>
The classed paths the change touches, for {classed_touched}.
.TP
.B \-\-description <text>
The file of the change's description, for {description}. Without it, /run/watchbill/description, where a session that wb start starts writes it.
.TP
.B \-\-brought <text>
The folder of what wb brings in for a session, for {brought}. Without it, /run/watchbill/in.
.TP
.B \-\-questions <text>
The file a session writes its questions to, for {questions}. Without it, /run/watchbill/questions.
.TP
.B \-\-change_comment <text>
The file the review writes its findings to and the fix its answers, for {change_comment}. Without it, /run/watchbill/change_comment.
.TP
.B \-\-needs_person <text>
The file a session writes what only a person can settle to, for {needs_person}. Without it, /run/watchbill/needs_person.
.TP
.B \-\-runner <name>
The runner the prompt is handed to. The planner needs it, and is handed out only to a runner that /etc/watchbill/organisation.yml clears for the planner up to the level of the repository, the line confidentiality: of watchbill.yml. Its stamp then has the model and effort of that file, and \-\-parts adds the level and the runner.
.TP
.B \-\-parts
Print the path and the SHA\-256 of each part, in the form sha256sum prints, in place of the text.
.TP
.B \-\-stamp
Print, in place of the text, what the assignment is: the role, its model and effort, the runner, the version and revision, the issue, the change, the round, the branch, the checkout, the time, and each part as \-\-parts gives it. Not with \-\-parts.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-origin
Print the whole prompt as it is, and before each part a line that says its kind (revision, issue, project), its path, where it was read from and its hash, the hash of its part line of the stamp. Below that line stands one line for each placeholder the part holds, and where its value came from. The text between the lines, put together, is what wb prompt prints without it. Not with \-\-parts or \-\-stamp.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Taken, and changes nothing: what the command prints is what a script reads.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The prompt is printed.
.TP
.B 1
It is refused.
.TP
.B 2
It was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.SH SEE ALSO
